<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=newspaper">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Newspaper</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1605">1605</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A newspaper is a periodical publication of written news about current events; the German-language Relation, printed from 1605 by Johann Carolus in Strasbourg, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, is often recognised as the first. Newspapers grew with the spread of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press, and within a few decades they could be found in all the major cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>; the first daily, the Einkommende Zeitung, appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in 1650. Much earlier, the Kaiyuan Za Bao, block-printed government news of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, is sometimes considered one of the earliest newspapers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/newspaper.jpg" alt="Newspaper" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: University library of Heidelberg, Germany · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relation_Aller_Fuernemmen_und_gedenckwuerdigen_Historien_(1609)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1605</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Strasbourg</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Johann Carolus · <a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Court gazettes circulated earlier in Tang China, and a printed daily, the Jobo, appeared in Joseon Korea in 1577.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">Printing press<span>c. 1440</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=newspaper">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
